# Distinct interactive states

On/off, pressed, selected, active, disabled, error, and similar states must be **obvious to humans at a glance** — not only different by a bit, boolean, or ARIA attribute.

## Do not rely on alone

- `aria-pressed`, `aria-selected`, or other ARIA flags without matching visuals
- Opacity tweaks of ~20% or less
- Font-weight change alone
- Tooltip or title text as the only indicator

## Prefer instead

Use enough **luminance and contrast** for dark UI so states read instantly:

- Fill or background change
- Border contrast (weight, color, or inset ring)
- Icon swap or glyph variant
- Clear label text when the control encodes mode

Combine cues when a single tweak is still subtle.

## Scope

Applies to toggles, segmented controls, cycling/mode buttons, toolbar chrome, and any control where the current state drives behavior or layout.

When reviewing or implementing UI, ask: *could someone tell the state in under a second without inspecting the DOM?* If not, strengthen the visual treatment.
